<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inisfallen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Innisfallen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inisfallen is an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-leane">Lough Leane</a>, the Lower Lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/killarney">Killarney</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kerry">County Kerry</a>, Ireland. A monastery was founded there, by tradition by Saint Finian the Leper in the 7th century, and it became a centre of learning where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-inisfallen">Annals of Inisfallen</a> were kept. Ruins of its church and later Augustinian priory survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inisfallen.jpg" alt="Inisfallen" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.-K. D. · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cloister_of_Innisfallen_Abbey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Innisfallen, in Irish Inis Faithlinn, Faithlinn's island, lies about midway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-leane">Lough Leane</a>. It covers some 8.5 hectares (21 acres), mostly wooded, with undulating hills and many slopes, and lies within Killarney National Park. The monastery is thought to have given the lake its name: Loch Léin is rendered in English as Lake of Learning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The monastery was founded around 640. Scholars identify its founder, traditionally a Saint Finian, either as Finian Lobhar, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>, or as Finan Cam of Corca Dhuibhne. Nothing survives of its earliest buildings, which were probably of wood. According to tradition the High King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a> was educated there under Maelsuthain O'Carroll, who has been credited as the possible originator of the Annals of Innisfallen. The Annals record Irish history from 433 to 1450, with further material on earlier times, and they tell of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids and of an attack in 1180 by the O'Donoghue clan, who carried off valuables from the church.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Norman invasion of Ireland</a> in the late 12th century the monastery came under Augustinian control and was renamed the Priory of Saint Mary. The community was dissolved in 1582 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, and the island passed through several hands before becoming part of the Kenmare estate. In 1973 it was transferred to the state and made part of Killarney National Park.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins date mainly from the 10th to the 12th centuries. Near the shore stands an oratory with a Romanesque doorway carved in a Hiberno-Norman style, and the larger church may reuse stone from earlier Irish-built churches. A small early church, domestic buildings of the monastery and a bullaun stone also survive.</p><p>The island appears in 19th-century works such as Thomas Moore's song Sweet Innisfallen and in William Allingham's poem The Abbot of Innisfallen, first published in Macmillan's Magazine in 1864. Geoffrey Molyneux Palmer's setting of the poem won the cantata prize at the 1908 Feis Ceoil in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, and Sean O'Casey took a line of Moore's, Inishfallen, Fare Thee Well, as the title of the fourth volume of his autobiography.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Innisfallen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Inisfallen</b> on Loch Lein became an important monastery, later home of the Annals of Inisfallen.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iarmumu">Kingdom of Iarmumu</a></li></ul></section>
